{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`java.security.KeyStore` represents a storage facility for cryptographic keys and certificates. Its entries include key and certificate entry types, and its persistence mechanism is implementation-dependent; the Javadoc allows implementations such as files, smart cards, and other cryptographic engines.","`InvalidSequenceOfMethodCalls` and the message “the observed call sequence is not one KeyStoreSpec accepts” are not documented `java.security.KeyStore` exceptions, return values, or API events. In the supplied CrySL source, the relevant condition is its `ORDER` expression: `Get, Load, ((GetEntry?, GetKey) | (SetEntry, Store))*`. Thus, this kind of finding refers to an analyzer’s modeled event trace failing that rule expression, rather than to a Java API value.","The supplied rule source declares `SPEC java.security.KeyStore`; its event patterns are consequently declared under that specification. The source itself does not define a global name-only match. It does not state that a method on an unrelated receiver type merely sharing one of these names is matched.","The exact matching clause in the supplied rule source is:
```text
EVENTS
	g1: getInstance(type);
	g2: getInstance(type, _);
	Get := g1 | g2;
	l1: load(fileInput, passwordIn);
	l2: load(paramLoad);
	Load := l1 | l2;

	s1: store(paramStore);
	s2: store(fileOutput, passwordOut);
	Store := s1 | s2;

	ge1: getEntry(aliasGet, protParamGet);
	GetEntry := ge1;

	se1: setEntry(aliasSet, entry, protParamSet);
	SetEntry := se1;

	gk1: key = getKey(alias, passwordKey);
	GetKey := gk1;
```
The declared receiver specification is `java.security.KeyStore`. The matched names are `getInstance`, `load`, `store`, `getEntry`, `setEntry`, and `getKey`. The event patterns constrain argument count and bind arguments to named rule variables; those variables have declarations in the source. In `getInstance(type, _)`, `_` is a wildcard, so that second argument is not named or type-constrained by that event expression itself.","The condition evaluated after those event definitions includes this exact order and constraint content:
```text
ORDER
	Get, Load, ((GetEntry?, GetKey) | (SetEntry, Store))*
CONSTRAINTS
	type in {"JCEKS", "JKS", "DKS", "PKCS11", "PKCS12"};
	neverTypeOf[passwordIn, java.lang.String];
	neverTypeOf[passwordOut, java.lang.String];
	neverTypeOf[passwordKey, java.lang.String];
	notHardCoded[passwordIn];
```
The raw rule also contains `ENSURES` declarations, but they are separate from this order condition.","The `KeyStore` Javadoc states that a keystore must normally be loaded before it can be accessed, unless it was already loaded when instantiated. For a type-based factory result, `load(InputStream, char[])` or `load(KeyStore.LoadStoreParameter)` is the documented initialization operation. Passing `null` as the input stream to `load` creates an empty keystore.","The Javadoc documents exceptions to the ordinary separate-load lifecycle: `KeyStore.getInstance(File, char[])` and `KeyStore.getInstance(File, KeyStore.LoadStoreParameter)` return a loaded keystore. Both overloads are documented since Java 9. The supplied CrySL event clause does not list either file-based overload.","For loaded keystores, the Javadoc documents `getKey`, `getEntry`, and `setEntry`; each can throw `KeyStoreException` when the keystore has not been initialized. `getKey` and `getEntry` may return `null` where the alias has no corresponding applicable entry. `getEntry` permits a null protection parameter.","The documented persistence APIs are `store(OutputStream, char[])` and `store(KeyStore.LoadStoreParameter)`. The stream-based store protects keystore integrity using the supplied password; a null password is permitted only where an integrity check is not supported or required by the implementation. `store(LoadStoreParameter)` may throw `UnsupportedOperationException` if storing is unsupported.","Official `KeyStore` documentation does not describe the CrySL diagnostic or prescribe one universal remediation for it. Its documented correction is limited to satisfying API prerequisites: use a loaded keystore before operations that require initialization; do not treat the Java-9 file-based `getInstance` overloads as unloaded; and use a store operation only where the selected keystore implementation supports storing.","The JCA Reference Guide states that keystore implementations of different types are not compatible. A keystore type determines its data format and the algorithms used to protect keys and keystore integrity. Therefore, changing keystore type or provider can affect persisted-format compatibility and protection behavior.","`KeyStore` does not define a network wire protocol. Oracle documents PKCS#12 as a cross-platform keystore format based on the PKCS#12 Personal Information Exchange Syntax Standard and intended for storage or transport of private keys, certificates, and other secrets; that is a keystore-format statement, not a `KeyStore` network-protocol definition.","The Java platform requires support for the `PKCS12` keystore type. Oracle documents PKCS12 as the default and recommended keystore type from JDK 9 onward, while the earlier default was proprietary JKS. Other keystore-type availability is provider- and implementation-dependent.","The documented migration mechanism is `keytool -importkeystore`, which imports an entire keystore, including keys and certificates, into another keystore and can be used across keystore types. Oracle also documents JCEKS support for parsing and converting JKS files. Neither source guarantees migration for every provider-specific entry, protection scheme, or non-recoverable key; `keytool` documents password recovery as a condition for importing private and secret keys."],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official API definition, initialization/loading requirements, file-based loaded factories, entry access, persistence semantics, and API version tags.","limitations":"Describes Java SE API semantics, not a particular provider’s implementation or the behavior of a CrySL analyzer.","claim":"`KeyStore` is a provider-based cryptographic key/certificate repository; normally it must be loaded before use unless instantiated already loaded; file-based factories return loaded keystores; and documented entry/store methods have the stated prerequisites and exceptions.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/security/KeyStore.html"},{"relevance":"Official Oracle JCA documentation on keystore-type compatibility, PKCS12, defaults, and JKS/JCEKS conversion.","limitations":"Statements about included/default Oracle JDK providers do not establish availability or behavior of every third-party or platform-specific provider.","claim":"Different keystore types are not compatible; PKCS12 is a cross-platform format and the JDK 9+ default/recommended type; JCEKS can parse and convert JKS files.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/security/java-cryptography-architecture-jca-reference-guide.html"},{"relevance":"Official `keytool` documentation for keystore import and migration limitations.","limitations":"Tool behavior depends on source/destination provider support, recoverable source material, and available credentials.","claim":"`keytool -importkeystore` imports a keystore’s keys and certificates into another keystore, subject to password recovery and conflict handling.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/man/keytool.html"},{"relevance":"Primary source for the declared CrySL specification, event patterns, order expression, and constraints quoted above.","limitations":"The URL points to the mutable `master` branch. The raw rule does not specify a detector release, type-resolution/dispatch behavior, handling of unresolved calls, or whether an individual reported call was resolved as `java.security.KeyStore`.","claim":"The rule declares `SPEC java.security.KeyStore`, defines the listed event patterns, and evaluates the quoted `ORDER` and `CONSTRAINTS` clauses.","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/KeyStore.crysl"}],"queries_performed":["Reviewed the supplied primary CrySL rule URL for its `SPEC`, `EVENTS`, `ORDER`, and `CONSTRAINTS` text.","Reviewed official Java SE `java.security.KeyStore` Javadoc for API lifecycle, loading, entry, store, and version semantics.","Reviewed the official Oracle JCA Reference Guide for keystore type compatibility, PKCS12, defaults, and conversion documentation.","Reviewed the official `keytool` manual for documented keystore migration behavior."],"externally_unresolved_questions":["The detector message alone does not identify the actual observed calls, their overloads, receiver-resolution result, object identity, control-flow path, or the event that made the trace fail. Therefore no individual call sequence can be classified from this message alone.","The raw CrySL file does not state the detector implementation’s exact matching semantics for inheritance, dynamic dispatch, unresolved types, wrappers, reflection, Kotlin, or interprocedural flows. Accordingly, whether a particular tool can match a same-named method in a specific codebase cannot be determined beyond the rule’s declared `SPEC java.security.KeyStore` scope.","The selected keystore provider, keystore type, Java/platform version, persistence destination, and entry/protection capabilities are not supplied. Those facts control support for particular formats, operations, and migrations.","The mutable `master`-branch rule URL does not establish the exact revision used by the reporting detector; the reported rule hash does not by itself identify a publicly verified source revision in the supplied evidence."]}
